<h1>Installation</h1>
<p class="lede">Install Booster Auth on a single machine with Docker Compose. The setup script generates every secret on your own server, and the
    same steps work for a trial on your laptop.</p>

<div class="code"><span class="lbl">In short · Linux / macOS</span><pre>unzip booster-auth.zip &amp;&amp; cd booster-auth
./setup.sh
docker compose up -d</pre></div>

<h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Docker Engine 24+ and Docker Compose v2 (Windows / macOS: Docker Desktop)</li>
    <li>At least 4 GB of free memory and 10 GB of disk</li>
    <li>Internet access during installation (images are pulled from <code>ghcr.io/booster-tr</code>)</li>
    <li>An address for Booster Auth, e.g. <code>auth.company.com</code>. It must be on <b>one of the domains in your license</b>.</li>
</ul>
<p>PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka and OpenFGA ship with the package; there is nothing else to install.</p>

<h2 id="download">1. Download the package</h2>
<p>Download <code>booster-auth.zip</code> (about 15 KB) from the <a href="/en/download">Download</a> page and unzip it:</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Linux / macOS</span><pre>unzip booster-auth.zip
cd booster-auth</pre></div>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Windows (PowerShell)</span><pre>Expand-Archive booster-auth.zip .
cd booster-auth</pre></div>

<h2 id="env">2. Run the setup script</h2>
<p>All settings live in the <code>.env</code> file. The easiest way to create it is the setup script: it asks for your addresses, generates every
    password and key at random and writes <code>.env</code>.</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Linux / macOS</span><pre>./setup.sh</pre></div>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">Windows</span><pre>powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\setup.ps1</pre></div>
<p>The script asks for the following:</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Prompt</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Booster Auth address (host)</td><td><code>auth.company.com</code></td><td>Where your applications send users to sign in; the OIDC issuer is built from it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HTTPS port</td><td><code>443</code></td><td>Use e.g. <code>8443</code> if 443 is taken.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Management panel port / address</td><td><code>3000</code> · <code>http://localhost:3000</code></td><td>The admin console address.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Administrator e-mail</td><td><code>it@@company.com</code></td><td>The first administrator's e-mail.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<h3>Filling it in by hand</h3>
<p>You can also skip the script, copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> and fill it in yourself:</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>PUBLIC_HOST</code>, <code>PUBLIC_URL</code>, <code>GATEWAY_HTTPS_PORT</code></td><td>The Booster Auth address. <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> must include any port other than 443.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>PANEL_PORT</code>, <code>PANEL_PUBLIC_URL</code></td><td>The admin console.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL</code>, <code>BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code></td><td>The first administrator. The username is always <code>admin</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code>, <code>REDIS_PASSWORD</code>, <code>OPENFGA_PRESHARED_KEY</code></td><td>Infrastructure passwords; long and random.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>HASHING_PEPPER</code>, <code>HASHING_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> (base64, 32 bytes), <code>IDP_APPLICATION_PEPPER</code></td><td>Password and secret encryption keys. <b>Never change them after installation</b>; existing passwords would become unreadable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>PANEL_SESSION_SECRET</code></td><td>Signs the console's session cookies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MAIL_*</code></td><td>Optional SMTP settings (verification, invitation and password reset e-mails).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BOOSTER_VERSION</code></td><td>Image version; default <code>latest</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout warn"><p><b>It will not start with placeholders.</b> If any secret still contains <code>CHANGE_ME</code>, the system refuses to
    start and names the variable. Back up <code>.env</code> and never share it.</p></div>

<h2 id="hosts">3. Map the address (local trial)</h2>
<p>If your address is not in DNS yet, add a line to your computer's <code>hosts</code> file:</p>
<div class="code"><span class="lbl">hosts</span><pre>127.0.0.1   auth.company.com</pre></div>
<p>Windows: <code>C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts</code> (open as administrator) · Linux / macOS: <code>/etc/hosts</code></p>

<h2 id="start">4. Start</h2>
<div class="code"><pre>docker compose up -d
docker compose ps</pre></div>
<p>The first start pulls the images and creates the databases; how long that takes depends mostly on your connection. When every service is
    <code>running</code>:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Admin console:</b> <code>http://localhost:3000</code> (or the address you entered)</li>
    <li><b>OIDC discovery:</b> <code>https://auth.company.com/idp/.well-known/openid-configuration</code></li>
</ul>
<p>These are the only two entry points. Services run with read-only filesystems, as non-root users and with Linux capabilities dropped;
    PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka and OpenFGA stay on the internal network.</p>

<h2 id="https">HTTPS certificate</h2>
<p>On first start, your own local certificate authority (CA) and a server certificate are generated (the <code>pki/</code> folder). Browsers do not
    know this CA and will show a warning:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Trial:</b> add <code>pki/trust/ca.crt</code> to your operating system as a trusted root certificate, or accept the warning.</li>
    <li><b>Production:</b> put a reverse proxy (nginx, Traefik, Cloudflare Tunnel) with your own certificate in front, or replace
        <code>pki/tls/server.crt</code> and <code>server.key</code> with your own certificate.</li>
    <li><b>Your own certificate:</b> the installation only renews the certificate it generated itself (signed by the local CA). It never touches a
        certificate you put there; if it does not contain the address (<code>PUBLIC_HOST</code>) or expires within 30 days, a warning is written to
        the log at start-up. Renewing it is up to you: put the new files in <code>pki/tls/</code> and run <code>docker compose restart booster-gateway</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Client IP behind a proxy:</b> by default the gateway trusts no forwarding header and uses the connection address (rate limiting,
    session list). If a load balancer or reverse proxy sits in front, set its network in <code>.env</code> so the real client IP is read:
    <code>TRUSTED_PROXY_NETWORK=10.0.0.0/8</code> (the proxy's address range). Behind Cloudflare also set
    <code>CLIENT_IP_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP</code>. Headers from untrusted addresses are ignored, so clients cannot fake their IP.</p>

<h2 id="first-sign-in">5. First sign-in</h2>
<p>Sign in to the console with the username <b><code>admin</code></b> and the initial password shown by the setup script; you will be asked to set
    a new one. The console starts <b>without a license</b>: end-user sign-ins work and management screens are read-only. Get a license on the
    <a href="/en/docs/licensing">Licensing</a> page, then continue with the <a href="/en/docs/first-steps">first configuration</a>.</p>
